      'A qualified retirement plan is one that satisfies the requirements of the Employee Retirement Income Security Act (ERISA). ERISA covers retirement plans offered by for-profit employers. Qualified employer-sponsored retirement plans come in two types: defined benefit plans and defined contribution plans.\n\nNonqualified plans would include Individual Retirement Accounts (IRAs) and deferred compensation plans.\n\nERISA Requirements ERISA requirements include:\n\n• Nondiscrimination: All employees must be treated equally under the act. These plans must be available to all eligible employees and not just highly paid executives.\n\nEligibility: Employers must offer their established plan to any employee who works more than 1,000 hours per year and has been with the company for more than 12 months. contributions are fully vested, the account is the property of the employee, even upon leaving the firm. There are two vesting schedules. With cliff vesting, employees are 100% vested after three years.\n\nWith graded vesting, employer contributions vest at 20% per year of service after the first year, with 100% vesting after six years.\n\nFiduciary responsibility: The plan trustee must manage the assets of the plan in the best interests of all participants. Plan assets must be segregated from other company assets.\n\nERISA covers plans offered by for-profit companies. It does not cover public retirement plans for not-for-profit entities or U.S. government employees or state employees. These plans are exempt from ERISA requirements.',
