      'Defined Benefit Plans',
      'A defined benefit plan is a qualified plan where the employer promises to pay each eligible employee a specific periodic (usually monthly) benefit for life, starting at retirement. A defined benefit plan, also called a pension plan, is typically funded and managed by the employer. This means the investment decisions are made by the employer, and it is the employer\'s responsibility to make sure that enough money is set aside for its employees\' retirement.\n\nDefined Contribution Plans\n\nA defined contribution plan is a retirement plan in which the employee determines the level of their contribution. Participants have their own individual accounts and choose from several different investment options. Benefits will depend on total contributions and investment returns.\n\nIn this kind of plan, participants typically contribute a portion of their earnings on a pre-tax basis. Often, employers will match a part of the employee\'s contributions. In a defined contribution plan, investment risk and investment rewards are assumed by the participant rather than by the employer.\n\nThe contributions and earnings grow on a tax-deferred basis until they are withdrawn, at which time they will be taxed as ordinary income. Contribution limits are set annually by the Internal Revenue Service (IRS). Some plans may allow participants aged 50 and above to make additional catch-up contributions.\n\nDefined contribution plan benefits may be paid out in a lump sum or in installments, or as an annuity over the employee\'s lifetime. The most common type of defined contribution plan is the 401(k) plan, a plan for corporate employees in which employees contribute a portion of their wages to individual accounts and where contributions may be matched by their employer.\n\nOther types of defined contribution plans include profit-sharing plans and 403(b) plans. Profit-sharing plans enable employers to contribute to employee accounts based on corporate profits. Contributions are not guaranteed and may be skipped in years in which the corporation is not profitable.\n\nEmployees of nonprofit entities may be offered 403(b) plans (also called tax-sheltered annuities), which are similar to 401(k) plans. These employers would include public schools and 501(c)(3) entities such as charities and religious organizations. The plans are exempt from ERISA requirements but often choose to follow them.\n\nWithdrawing funds from defined contribution plans: While individuals can withdraw funds at any time from their defined contribution account, they will be taxed and receive an additional 10% tax penalty on any withdrawals made before age 59 1/2. The early withdrawal penalty can be avoided if the withdrawal is for any of the following reasons:\n\n• The payment of medical expenses that exceed 7.5% of the individual\'s gross income • Death or permanent disability • The withdrawals occur in five substantially equal periodic payments • The distribution is made to an employee after separation from service, as long as the separation occurred\n• The payment of medical expenses that exceed 7.5% of the individual\'s gross income\n• Death or permanent disability\n\nThe withdrawals occur in five substantially equal periodic payments The distribution is made to an employee after separation from service, as long as the separation occurred during or after the calendar year in which the employee reached age 55\n\n• A person who has a child (including by adoption) can withdraw up to $5,000 from their 401(k) or IRA without penalty. This benefit must be used within one year of the date of birth or adoption, and the $5,000 limit applies separately to each parent',
