      'Under Internal Revenue Service (IRS) rules, individuals can set up individual retirement accounts (IRAs) to save for retirement on a tax-deferred basis. (You might also see IRAs referred to as individual retirement arrangements, terminology often used by the IRS.) Depending on income and availability of a workplace retirement plan, certain individuals may deduct their contributions. At withdrawal, all deductible (pre-tax) contributions and earnings are taxed at the individual\'s ordinary tax rate.\n\nNondeductible (after-tax) contributions are tax-free. To distinguish from Roth IRAs, these accounts are often referred to as traditional IRAs.\n\nContributing to an IRA\n\nThe government puts limits on IRA contributions. All contributions must be made only from earned income, as opposed to passive income, which will be discussed in a later section. Earned income typically means wages.\n\n(Unearned income includes dividends, capital gains, and interest on securities; Social Security benefits; unemployment benefits; child support; rental income; and inherited funds.) Contribution Limits Traditional IRA contributions are limited to earned income.',
